
CS 214 Project III - minimal shell example
Partner 1 name / NetID: FILL IN
Partner 2 name / NetID: FILL IN

BUILD (Linux / iLab)
make
./mysh                 interactive when stdin is a terminal
./mysh commands.txt    batch file
make sanitize          optional sanitizer build; repeat manual tests afterward
make clean

DESIGN
One read()-based input loop and parser serve both modes. One-byte reads avoid
reading beyond a newline before executing the command. Lines and arguments grow
dynamically. Batch children use /dev/null unless input is redirected or piped.
Wildcards use opendir/readdir with prefix/suffix matching. Unmatched patterns
stay literal. Patterns beginning with * exclude hidden names.
Lookup uses access and /usr/local/bin, /usr/bin, /bin in that order.
Standalone built-ins execute in the parent with saved/restored descriptors.
Pipelines launch all children before waiting and close unused descriptors.
Every child is reaped; the last stage determines status. Pipeline built-ins run
in children. The parent detects exit and stops after the entire job completes.
Output creation uses mode 0640 subject to umask. Programs execute through execv.

SCOPE
Uses the notes on dynamic memory, directory IO, processes, pipes and wait status.
Networking is unnecessary. No quoting, variables or background jobs are required.
Operators must have surrounding spaces. One star in the last path component is
supported. Wildcard order follows directory enumeration, not sorting.
Repeated redirection of the same stream is treated as a syntax error.
The contradictory mode sentence is interpreted using the examples: argument
files are batch; otherwise isatty selects the mode. Input redirection changes
stdin, output redirection changes stdout. Pipeline redirections are supported
for the exit example; explicit redirection overrides the pipe endpoint.

MANUAL TEST PLAN
No Python or automated test framework is used. Compare outputs by hand and record
actual observations. Run setup in your REGULAR Linux shell from mysh-example:
  make
  mkdir test-work
  cd test-work
  touch one.txt two.txt .hidden.txt foo foobar
  mkdir sub
  touch sub/a.txt
  umask 022
Use a fresh test-work directory to keep wildcard results predictable.

1. Run ../mysh ../tests/basic.in
Expected output, in order:
  hello
  after-cat
  first
  second
  replacement
  50000 (possibly with leading spaces)
  absolute path of test-work
  same absolute path of test-work
  one.txt two.txt (either order)
  absent*
  foobar
  one.txt two.txt foobar (first two may be reversed)
  sub/a.txt
  first executable echo in the three search directories
  /bin/cat
  first executable which in the three search directories
No prompts, welcome or goodbye should appear. cat must not consume subsequent
commands. The large three-stage pipeline must finish without hanging.
The paths check redirected pwd and that pipeline cd leaves the parent unchanged.
In your regular shell, cat data must print replacement; cat copy prints first
and second; ls -l data must show rw-r----- for the newly created file.
Repeat using cat ../tests/basic.in | ../mysh; expect the same results.

2. Run ../mysh ../tests/errors.in
Expect diagnostics and a final stdout line of alive. Immediately run echo $?
in your regular shell: expect 0.
Run ../mysh nonexistent and immediately echo $?: expect nonzero and a diagnostic.
In your regular shell:
  touch blocked
  chmod 000 blocked
  printf 'echo x > blocked\necho recovered\n' | ../mysh
As a normal non-root user, expect a permission error followed by recovered.
  chmod 644 blocked
  printf './blocked\necho recovered\n' | ../mysh
Expect an execution error followed by recovered.

3. Run ../mysh ../tests/exit.in
Expect no stdout. In your regular shell, cat farewell must print farewell;
unreachable must never appear. This checks completion before exit.
  printf 'exit ignored\n' | ../mysh
Expect silent successful exit.
  printf 'echo final' | ../mysh
Expect final despite the missing trailing newline.
  printf '' | ../mysh
Expect no output.

4. Start ../mysh without arguments. Expect welcome and an immediate prompt
ending in '$ '. Enter each command separately:
  cd
  pwd
  cd /
  pwd
  cd /does-not-exist-987
  pwd
  false
  false | true
  true | false
  which
  exit anything
After cd alone expect ~$ and HOME from pwd. After cd / expect /$ and /.
Failed cd leaves the directory unchanged. false reports status 1;
false | true reports no failure; true | false reports status 1. which without
arguments fails. exit ignores arguments and prints goodbye.
Repeat with cd to a HOME subdirectory: expect ~/subdirectory. If available,
visit a directory outside HOME sharing its text prefix: expect its full path.
Start again and press Ctrl-D at an empty prompt: expect goodbye.

5. In your regular shell, from test-work:
  gcc -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -o signal-helper ../tests/signal.c
  ../mysh
Inside mysh, enter cat, type hello and Enter, then press Ctrl-D. cat echoes hello
and the prompt returns. Enter ./signal-helper: expect a signal description and
another prompt. Enter exit. Back in your regular shell:
  printf './signal-helper\necho alive\n' | ../mysh
Expect alive with no interactive signal-status banner.

6. In a text editor, create long.in containing echo, a space and at least 2,000 x
characters on one line. Run ../mysh long.in: expect the complete line without a
crash. Include this additional input in your submission if used.
Optionally return to mysh-example, run make sanitize, then repeat the manual
cases. Expect no AddressSanitizer or UBSan diagnostics. This target only builds.

VALIDATION STATUS
Source and instructions reviewed on Windows. No usable Linux C compiler or WSL
was available. Compilation, runtime tests and sanitizer checks have NOT been
performed. These are expected results, not claims of passing tests. Run the plan
on iLab, record observations and fill in partner information before submission.
The example targets the stated requirements but cannot guarantee full marks.

PACKAGING
From the containing directory:
  tar -czf mysh.tar.gz mysh-example/mysh.c mysh-example/Makefile \
      mysh-example/README mysh-example/tests
Include any additional test inputs actually used. No Python tests or generated
executables are included in the supplied archive.
